WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:05.170
A fraction of a second ago, the electricity
powering your lights, your air conditioning,

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:09.889
your television, your refrigerator, nearly
everything electric around you right now,

00:00:09.889 --> 00:00:15.769
was water, gas, wind, or some other form of
energy, potentially hundreds or thousands

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:17.160
of miles away.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.270
If you flip a light switch in Los Angeles,
that could cause a turbine in Washington to

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.050
start spinning.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:28.500
Electric grids are considered some of the
single largest machines in existence—they

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:34.260
stretch across entire continents—but they’re
machines with absolutely zero slack.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.430
With water systems, for example, there’s
some extra supply in a storage reservoir at

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:42.170
the treatment plant, and some extra supply
in the pipes connecting the treatment plant

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:46.880
to your home—supply only has to roughly
match demand—but that’s not the case with

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:47.880
electricity.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:53.719
Your lights, your TV, your microwave—everything
plugged into the grid uses electricity produced

00:00:53.719 --> 00:00:55.079
just moments ago.

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:59.129
For the sake of example, let’s look at what
it takes to turn on the lights at the public

00:00:59.129 --> 00:01:03.129
library in the small town of Glenwood Springs,
Colorado.

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:08.250
Now, what makes the relatively straightforward
task of converting natural fuels or phenomena

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:13.520
into electric power difficult is the huge
variability in demand from minute-to-minute

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.000
and month-to-month.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.040
There are, however, some trends.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.940
On an annual basis, the peaks correlate to
climate—January represents the winter high-water

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:27.130
mark as many homes and businesses turn on
their electric heating, while July typically

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:31.600
ranks highest outright as the country battles
summer heat with air conditioners.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.010
The month-to-month variations are not that
significant, though—the difference between

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:39.880
the highest and lowest demand months is less
than a third.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.200
Most of the variability happens across a given
day.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.340
In January, all regions of the US observe
a two-peak daily demand trend, where electricity

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:53.040
use spikes around 7:00 AM as people turn up
their thermostats and fire up lights and appliances

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.549
as they wake up and get ready for the day.

00:01:55.549 --> 00:02:00.039
Then, demand peaks again when people return
from work in the evening and do the same.

00:02:00.039 --> 00:02:04.869
Meanwhile, in July, energy use is highest
in the late-afternoon or early-evening, but

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:07.060
this does depend on the region.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:12.020
For example, in the sweltering Southwest,
usage peaks around 4:00 pm—around the hottest

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.450
hour of the afternoon, when air conditioners
have to work the hardest.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.690
In the more temperate northwest, though, which
includes Colorado and that Glenwood Springs

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:24.791
public library, fewer buildings have air conditioning
so demand peaks around 6:00 pm—pulled back

00:02:24.791 --> 00:02:29.050
by post-work use of appliances, lights, televisions,
and more.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:33.670
These trends then get further complicated
by holidays and special events—for example,

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:38.650
on Superbowl Sunday in the US, demand plummets
for a few hours as people turn off the lights,

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:42.690
stop cooking, and gather together at others’
homes to watch the big game.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:48.030
This is all to say, when forecasting demand,
utilities need to factor in the hour, day,

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.950
season, location, and plenty more.

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:57.540
Now, with whatever demand materializes, utilities
then need to essentially instantly match supply.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.480
To do this, they layer different energy sources
on top of each other.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:05.250
Often, the lowest layer is Nuclear Power.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:09.670
Nuclear power stations aren’t designed to
be able to turn off and on easily—it would

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:14.140
take the better part of a day to shutdown
and start up again—so it’s rather impractical

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.630
to operate these in response to demand.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:21.790
In addition, considering how massively expensive
their construction is, it would economically

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.500
inefficient to let these stations run idle.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:29.600
Therefore, they’ll run nearly continuously
for the entirety of their service lives, except

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.480
for during planned maintenance.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.920
This means they provide the most stable supply
of electricity 24/7.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:42.020
Colorado no longer operates any nuclear power
plants, but it does have six of the next layer—coal

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:43.500
power stations.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:47.800
Like nuclear, most of these coal plants cannot
be fired up quickly, as they take some time

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:52.620
to get up to temperature, so utilities will
also run them essentially continuously.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:56.720
Coal and nuclear combined typically fulfill
what’s called the base-load.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.560
Their continuous production essentially equals
the valley of demand—they’ll make the

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.710
minimum amount of power needed at a point
in the day.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:10.440
So then, on top of that, utilities need production
methods that can fire up and shut down far

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:15.350
faster—they need to be able to produce the
power to operate all those AC units in hot

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:17.060
summer afternoons.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:21.310
The bulk of this job is completed by natural
gas fired plants.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:27.970
Of the 25 in Colorado, 13 of these stations
generate using a simple cycle combustion turbine.

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:33.120
This is the simplest means of turning natural
gas into electricity—much like a jet engine,

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.000
the fuel is used to turn a turbine, which
turns a generator, which produces electricity.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.660
More advanced natural gas power plants recover
the hot exhaust to convert it into electricity

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:47.120
too, but the startup process for these takes
longer, so simple cycle stations are used

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.120
for applications where speed is key.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:54.260
Most can reach full power within 15 minutes,
making them an effective tool to respond to

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.050
those short peaks in demand.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:02.220
Nuclear, coal, and natural gas are some of
the more traditional means of generating electricity.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:07.300
The recent proliferation of renewable energy
sources, though, has kickstarted an reinvention

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.130
of this system of base-load and peaker plants.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:15.700
That’s because, when wind and solar generating
stations can make power, that power is essentially

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:21.420
free—meaning there’s essentially no additional,
variable cost to generate power once the panels

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:23.070
or turbines are installed.

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:28.160
That’s not the case with natural gas fired
plants, for example, as the gas itself represents

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.200
one of their primary costs.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:34.790
So, this essentially creates a new bottom
layer to the energy mix—but it’s a bottom

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:37.750
layer that’s uncontrollably variable.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:42.420
One sunny, windy day could mean that a state
has an excess of electricity when added to

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:46.890
its base-load sources, while one cloudy, windless
day could mean that the peaker plants have

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:49.120
to operate at full force.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:53.640
Solar and onshore wind power are now some
of the cheapest sources of electricity, but

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:58.260
because they rely on natural phenomena, they
can’t match supply to demand the way that

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.990
the traditional nuclear, coal, natural gas
trifecta can.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:07.010
Therefore, grids are being reinvented so that
supply doesn’t have to match demand—at

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:08.010
least minute-to-minute.

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:14.580
Of course, the way to do that is with storage,
but storing electricity is not easy.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:19.530
The average American household uses about
30 kilowatt-hours of electricity per day.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:24.900
Lithium-ion batteries currently cost around
$130 per kilowatt-hour, meaning one-day’s

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:29.870
storage for one household would cost around
$3,900 in batteries—and that’s excluding

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:34.330
all the additional infrastructure needed to
make such an installation possible.

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:38.370
Considering the average American household
pays less than half that per year in electricity

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:42.930
bills, installing such a system grid-wide
would be economically impractical.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:48.420
Nonetheless, Colorado has four battery-electric
storage systems—albeit it at a relatively

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:49.630
minor scale.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:55.050
The largest of which is used to power Fort
Carson—a US army base near Colorado Springs.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:59.820
They charge up the 8.5 megawatt battery at
night, when electricity demand, and therefore

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:04.050
rates, are low, then use the power during
the day when the majority of the base’s

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:05.510
electricity use occurs.

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:10.010
The system’s savings should make up for
its installation cost in about 13 years, then

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:13.950
by the end of its practical lifespan it’s
expected to have saved the army about half

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:16.950
a million dollars in electricity costs.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:21.190
While such a system can make economic sense
for large complexes that can invest in infrastructure

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:25.670
that won’t pay off for more than a decade,
it’s just not practical grid-wide—at least

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:26.670
right now.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:30.919
Even if there was the battery production capacity
to make a meaningful dent in the world’s

00:07:30.919 --> 00:07:35.280
electricity use, the savings just happen too
far in the future for most to consider such

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.090
storage to be worth it.

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:41.830
However, for entirely different reasons, in
the coming decades, more and more people will

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:46.960
be purchasing massive battery packs—the
ones inside their EVs.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:51.170
With most forecasts suggesting that some third
of new car sales in the US will be electric

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:56.430
by 2030, a potential solution to the grid’s
storage need could arise naturally.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:03.570
A Rivian R1T, for example, holds a 135 kilowatt-hour
battery pack, meaning it could, theoretically,

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:07.270
power the average American household for four
and a half days.

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:12.330
While EV’s typically take in electricity,
little stands in the way technologically from

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.800
them giving that electricity back to the grid.

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:19.440
So, the idea is that EV’s could charge at
night or when renewables are performing well,

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.010
then supply some of that electricity back
to the grid during the day while parked and

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:24.010
plugged in.

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:28.660
In exchange, vehicle owners would be compensated
a small amount by the utility company.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:32.909
While the real-world, wide-scale economics
have yet to be proven, especially in the face

00:08:32.909 --> 00:08:37.620
of battery degradation, this vehicle-to-grid
concept could potentially play a cooperative

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:40.460
role in the transition towards a greener grid.

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:45.080
Now, the largest current battery electric
storage system in Colorado, the Fort Carson

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:48.260
installation, has 8.5 megawatts of capacity.

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:53.740
Elsewhere in the state, however, there’s
a massive, different form of battery can can

00:08:53.740 --> 00:09:00.070
power over 10,000 homes at once—and it looks
like this.

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:04.410
Water is one of the earliest forms of energy
that humans harnessed, and it still represents

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:08.180
one of the best sources of electricity.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:10.220
Hydroelectric power is the best of both worlds.

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:14.500
While there’s certainly the potential for
environmental damage from damming up rivers,

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:19.300
the carbon impact is near-zero, meaning hydro
is green like wind and solar.

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:21.880
However, it’s also reliable.

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:26.900
In theory, hydroelectric dams can start and
stop making power in minutes—even if in

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:32.260
practice a minimum flow is typically maintained—so
they can operate like those natural gas, single-cycle

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:34.420
peaker plants, responding to demand.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:39.570
Of course, there’s only so much water upstream—especially
in a place like Colorado, which is in the

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:45.650
midst of a 20-year-long drought—but hydroelectric
dams simply convert potential energy into

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:46.650
electric energy.

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:50.300
There’s no reason why that process can’t
happen in reverse.

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:54.870
That’s what happens here, at the Cabin Creek
Generating Station.

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:58.370
When electricity’s cheap—typically at
night, or when wind and solar are performing

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:03.460
at their peak—the facility uses it to pump
water to its upper, higher-elevation reservoir.

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:08.240
Then, when electricity’s expensive and in
demand, like in the late afternoon, it will

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.190
start flowing that water through its turbines,
out into its lower reservoir.

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:16.520
It’s essentially a form of price arbitrage,
but its one that’s already helping smooth

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:20.240
out the peaks and valleys of wind and solar
power production.

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:25.589
Now, no matter how well supply is matched
to demand, electricity is of no use to that

00:10:25.589 --> 00:10:30.640
Glenwood Springs, Colorado library until,
of course, it gets there.

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:35.070
Many have an image of power plants as something
sitting just outside every town and city.

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:39.580
While that’s how the earliest grids operated,
it’s hardly the case anymore.

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:44.930
Despite dramatic declines in the past decade,
some 42% of Colorado’s power supply still

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:46.630
comes from coal.

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:50.960
That means that nearly half of the state’s
electricity originates from, essentially,

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:54.610
just six sites—the state’s coal power
stations.

00:10:54.610 --> 00:11:00.270
Therefore, the ability to efficiently transport
electricity over a long distance is essential.

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:06.340
Now, what makes long-distance transmission
tricky is simple: the further you send electricity,

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:10.900
the more you lose along the way—typically
through unavoidable conversion to heat.

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:15.700
Meanwhile, the more centralized your electricity
production, the less it costs—it’s simple

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:17.630
economies of scale.

00:11:17.630 --> 00:11:22.210
Therefore, the task of transmission is all
about balancing these competing effects to

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:23.790
find an equilibrium.

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:29.500
Now, houses, businesses, and other buildings
connected to the electric grid all use alternating

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:31.040
current power.

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:35.320
A big reason why this standard beat out its
direct current competitor in the days of Tesla,

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:40.100
Westinghouse, and Edison is because it’s
relatively easy to convert AC power between

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:43.220
higher and lower voltages using transformers.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:48.399
What makes this crucial is that the higher
the voltage, the less electricity lost during

00:11:48.399 --> 00:11:49.399
transmission.

00:11:49.399 --> 00:11:54.600
Specifically, a doubling in voltage quarters
the power loss in transmission.

00:11:54.600 --> 00:12:00.170
That relatively simple voltage transformation
allows for a more efficient electricity distribution

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:01.170
system.

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:06.730
Colorado’s second largest power plant is
this: the Craig Generating Station.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:10.820
It connects to some of the state’s highest
voltage transmission lines—two of which

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:13.740
head due south until the town of Rifle.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:20.830
Now, these lines operate at 345 kilovolts,
which means that, in theory, over the 75 mile

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:27.230
or 120 kilometer stretch from Craig to Rifle,
about 3.2% of the electricity is lost—a

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:31.960
relatively minor amount, thanks to the efficiency
of high-voltage transmission.

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:36.370
Other more densely populated regions, which
need more electric transmission capacity,

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:41.670
have even higher voltage lines, all the way
up to 765 kilovolts—the current highest

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:44.360
voltage transmission standard in the US.

00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:49.310
Quite few of these lines exist in the US—there’s
one connecting Quebec to New York State, and

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:52.550
then a larger system spanning between Illinois
and Virginia.

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:58.240
These can carry about six times as much electricity
as the 345 kilovolt line between Craig and

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:06.339
Rifle and, thanks to their high voltage, see
as little as 0.6% loss per 100 miles or 160

00:13:06.339 --> 00:13:07.459
kilometers.

00:13:07.459 --> 00:13:13.520
That means that, in theory, assuming the infrastructure
existed, a 765 kilovolt line could transmit

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:18.730
electricity from New York to LA, and about
87% of it would still be there at the other

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:19.730
end.

00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:23.640
That’s why these high-voltage lines are
becoming more and more common.

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:28.680
But what’s even more efficient than transmitting
an alternating current at a super high voltage

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:30.960
is transmitting a direct current.

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:34.750
Of course, the difficulty with direct current
is that transforming it between voltages is

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:39.110
more expensive than with AC, and for use in
today’s grid, it has to be converted to

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:41.470
AC power, which is also expensive.

00:13:41.470 --> 00:13:47.170
However, compared to the absolute lowest 0.6%
loss per 100 miles with the highest voltage

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:53.500
AC lines, DC lines see as little as 0.45%
loss per 100 miles.

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:58.519
Therefore, when there’s a need to transmit
a huge amount of electricity over a long distance,

00:13:58.519 --> 00:14:03.170
the economics can work in favor of converting
power to DC at its origin, and converting

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:06.050
it back to AC at its destination.

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:10.290
Perhaps the most prominent application of
such a system in the US is the Pacific DC

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:14.899
Intertie, stretching between northern Oregon
and Los Angeles, California.

00:14:14.899 --> 00:14:19.250
In the summer, the temperate northwest has
relatively little power demand, but generates

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:23.519
a huge amount of cheap electricity thanks
to its hydroelectric dams.

00:14:23.519 --> 00:14:29.430
So, the Pacific DC Intertie sends this electricity
south to power Los Angeles’ air conditioners

00:14:29.430 --> 00:14:31.310
during its hot summer.

00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:35.730
Meanwhile, in the winter, Los Angeles sees
temperate weather and little need for climate

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:40.510
control, so it sends its excess electricity
north to the Pacific Northwest to power its

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:42.180
electric heating.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:46.450
Phenomena like this demonstrate why creating
a greener, more efficient grid involves a

00:14:46.450 --> 00:14:51.370
proliferation of long-distance power transmission
infrastructure—its needed to balance out

00:14:51.370 --> 00:14:57.029
seasonal trends, and to bring power from where
it can be produced easily to where it cannot.

00:14:57.029 --> 00:15:02.540
Back in Colorado, though, its relatively minor
345 kilovolt line from the Craig Power Station

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:06.670
eventually enters this more-populated area
flanking Interstate 70.

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:12.769
Here, there are a number of nearby towns that
need electricity, but sending 345 kilovolts

00:15:12.769 --> 00:15:15.589
to each of these would not be efficient.

00:15:15.589 --> 00:15:20.470
That’s simply because the higher the voltage,
the higher the construction cost.

00:15:20.470 --> 00:15:27.990
A 345 kilovolt substation might cost $10.7
million, while a 230 kilovolt one just $7.6

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:28.990
million.

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:35.579
Meanwhile, a 345 kilovolt line costs around
$4.5 million per mile to build, while a 230

00:15:35.579 --> 00:15:38.180
kilovolt line just $2.8 million.

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:42.450
Therefore, even though the transmission losses
will be greater, a number of substations around

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:47.670
Rifle transform the electricity to 230 kilovolts,
and then smaller, cheaper lines carry the

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:51.690
flow west, south, and east, closer to its
final customers.

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:57.120
About 30 miles or 50 kilometers to the east,
this line encounters another substation, which

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:02.029
steps it down to just 69 kilovolts and then
another yet smaller line circles back towards

